@stephentoub Let me be more clear here, the attribute will be added on a non-partial local function, which is a valid syntax.

Currently, there is:

```csharp
                        // Scope to just methods
                        if (member is not MethodDeclarationSyntax methodSyntax)
                        {
                            continue;
                        }
```

which seems to silently ignore the attribute for that case. My expectation is to produce a diagnostic for that. Or if you want to silently skip it, then it's at least tested.

@stephentoub I'm referring to a non-partial local function, which compiles fine:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Method, AllowMultiple = false)]
public class MyAttribute : Attribute
{
}

public class C
{
    public void M()
    {
        Abc();

        [My]
        static Regex Abc() => null;        
    }
}
```

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/31348972/133617929-6b914748-0849-4849-a137-1e8a160a3799.png)

Do we want this to be just skipped as if the attribute isn't there? or do we want a diagnostic? whatever the desired behavior, it's good to be tested. I know this case isn't really important and both behaviors could be reasonable. But just for sake of completeness.
